1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bd3 Nc6 5. c3 Qc7 6. h3 Nf6! {The most flexible move and now White must decide what to do with his knight on g1:} (6. Nf3 {After 6.Nf3 Of course, the bishop must be developed.} g6! 7. O-O {looks like good prophylaxis against Black's planned ...f5, but we have a crafty trick up our sleeve: after castling we can play ...h5-f4 and chase away the bishop.} Bg7 8. O-O {leads to:} O-O (8... h5 {I like that even after 10 Bg5 that Black can stick to the plan with 10...h5.} 9. Qd2 f6 10. Be3 e5 11. g4 e4! 12. Ne2 (12. gxh5 exd3 13. Qh6 Kh8 14. Ng2 b6 15. Ne1 Ne7 16. Nxd3 Qe6 17. Kh1 f5 White is struggling with his open king)) 12... exf3 13. Nxf3 f5 14. gxh5 f4 15. Nxd5+ Kh8 16. hxg6 Bxh3 17. Bg2 Bxg2 18. Kxg2 Nf3+ White was being killed in D.Pershin-M.Matlakov, St Petersburg 2013. Impressive play.)) 9. Wc2) 7. Nd2 {White does not control any central squares so our reaction is standard:} e5!. {Black should not worry about the resulting IQP since he will have easy piece play. We should also bear in mind that White's weaknesses are on the b1-h7 diagonal and note that we have strong squares on e4 and c4. These may become important later on.} 8. dxe5 Nxe5 9. Qc2 c5 10. Qb3 {E.Geller-Y.Razuvaev, USSR Championship, Minsk 1979} Nd6 11. Nf3 O-O 12. O-O Re8 13. Be3 (13. Bg5 Ne4 14. Be3 Nc4 15. Qxd5 Bf6 16. Qd4 Nxe3 17. fxe3 Re6 when Black's two bishops along with the heavy pressure against the weak e3-pawn give him good compensation for the pawn) 13... Nd7 14. Bxf5 Nxf5 15. Qd3 a6 {is equal.}) (7. Qe2?? Nxd4 {nets a pawn.}) 7. Ne2 {This is aimed at making ...g6 less favourable as Black will not be able to swap off the bad light-squared bishop. However, White is relinquishing control over the e5-square, so I believe Black should abandon his strategic ideas and opt for active play in the centre. Remember: No control over the centre? Play...e7-e5!} e5! 8. dxe5 Nxe5 9. Qc2 {White here has a variety of other moves to choose from:} Bd6 10. Bg5 (10. Be3?! Bd6 11. Nd4 a6! (there is no way we will ever allow White to play b5, exchanging the bishop) 12. O-O O-O 13. Qc2 (if 12. Bf5 Bxf5 13. Bxf5 Nc4) 12... Nc4! (as mentioned before, Black makes use of the strong squares) 13. Rc1 Re8 14. Bd2 b5 15. Be3 Nb8! (with the beautiful idea of a rook lift along the sixth rank to aid a potential kingside attack; instead, 16...b7 was played in B. Giulio-A. Rasmussen, Dresden Olympiad 2008) 17. Rb1 Rb6 18. b3 Ne5 19. Nxe5 Bxe5 and Black has tremendous piece coordination) (9. Nb5+ {can be met with the natural} Bd7 10. Nxd7+ Qxd7 11. Ng3 Bd6 12. O-O O-O 13. Bg5 Ne4! 14. Bxe4 dxe4 15. Nd2 f5 and Black has not only the superior pawn structure, but an outpost on d3 for his knight, A.Bilgin-R.Bigaliev, Istanbul 2010.) (9. f4 {is, of course, met with} Nd6 10. Nxe5 Nxe5 11. Nd2 O-O 12. O-O Rh2+ (standard technique to push the king one square away) 13. Kh1 Nd6 14. Nd4 a6 15. Re1 Rd7 Jobava now handled the black side of the position very impressively: 16. Qf3 Rae8 17. Nf5 e5 (17...Nxf5 18.Bxf5 Qb6 would be a safer method) 18. Bg3 g6 19. Rfd4 Qb6 20. Re2 Rb8 21. Rxe8 Rxe8 22. Bf1 Nh5 23. Nc5 Bxh3 24. a4 Qd6 25. Qxh3 e4 26. Ng1 Qf4 27. Nf3 Ra7 0-1, E.Krivoborodov-B.Jobava, Moscow 2008.) (9. Nd4 {doesn't really promise anything. Black may have an isolated pawn, but he has a strong square for his knight on e4 and the bishops:} Nxd3+ 10. Qxd3 c5 11. O-O O-O 12. Be3 a6 13. Nd2 Re8 14. Rfe1 Nd7 15. Qc2 Rd6 16. Bd4 (16. Bg5 b5 17. Nf3 e4 is slightly better for Black too) 16... Nh5 and although the game ended in a draw in A.Semeniuk-P.Maletin, Vladivostok 2012, Black stands a bit better on account of his superior piece activity.) Ne4 11. Be3 O-O {A cool retort.} (11... Nf6 {and} 11... e6 {are more boring ways of playing the position.}) 12. Bxd5 (12. f4 Nf5 13. Nxd5 c6 14. Nf4 Nc4 with advantage to Black.) Nf6 13. Nd4 Nc4 14. Bg5 e5 15. Qh4 h6 16. Bxf6 Bxf6 17. Qe4 g6 18. Qb3 (18. O-O?? {which drops a piece to} 18... Nf5.) b5! 19. Bxa8 Rb7 20. Qxa7 (20. Qxf8+ Kxf8 21. O-O Rxb2 22. Nd2 Qd7 and the knight on d2 doesn't have a good home.) Ra8 21. Qxa8+ Kxa8 Again Black's lead in development gives him a very pleasant position. 22. O-O Rxb2 23. Nd2 Qd7 Black wins back his material with the upper hand.
