1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bd3 Nc6 5. c3 Qc7 6. Bg5 {I would describe this as the classical approach. White is primarily interested in achieving good development and securing harmonious squares for his knights.} Nf6! {This move may be surprising since Black voluntarily offers to give himself doubled pawns, but should White choose to exchange he will be giving Black a helping hand in establishing a nice pawn centre. White has played several moves here:} (6. f3 {transposes to Line B2, as, indeed, would} Nf6 7. Bg5 Ng4 8. Nbd2) (6. h3 {Now I think that Black should be bold and strike out:} e5!? (7... h6 {followed by} ...e7-e5 {is also acceptable}) 8. dxe5 Qxe5+ 9. Qe2 Nc5 10. Nxe5+ Nxe5 11. Nb5+ Bd7, and if 12. Nxf6 gxf6 13. Qe2 Ng8 Black will follow up with ...d5-d4.) (6. Qb3 {is also met with the pawn push} e5! (the rule, the rule!) 7. Nd2 (8. dxe5 {hands Black the advantage after} Nxe5+ 9. Ne3 Nc5! and 8. Qe2?! {leaves the white bishop on d3 with no good squares; after} e4 9. Nb5 a6 10. Bxc6+ bxc6 Black has a nibble based on the bishop-pair) e4! 8. Nc2 e6 and White has problems with his queen.) (6. Qh4 {I like} e5 {as} 8. Ng3 {doesn't really pose a problem because of} d6.) (6. Ne2 {allows Black to develop the bishop, so we should play} Bg4 7. O-O e6 8. Qc2 h5! 9. Re1 (10. Nd2 {is a mistake because of} Nxe2 11. Nxe2 Nxd4 12. Qa4+ Nc6) Bg6 10. Nd2 Be7 with no problems.)
7. Bxf6 {White fails to prevent Black's planned break, but the damage has already been done.} (7. Nbd2 Bg4 8. Ngf3 White plays in a very classical manner, but Black too has a very simple objective: neutralize the b1-h7 diagonal and play for a minority attack. We should also consider: (8. Qb1 {has the aim of swapping the g4-bishop for a knight, but it's not so logical. I believe the best way to play against this is to follow the rule:} e5! 9. Nh3 Nd7 10. dxe5 Nxe5+ 11. Ne3 Nc5 12. Ngf3 Qe7 13. O-O (13. Qd1 Nxe3 14. Ke1 O-O 15. Nxe3 Qd6 16. Ne1 d4 when Black was doing very well in G.Sarakauskas-R.Ovetchkin, Plovdiv 2008) Nxe3 14. Re1 Nxf2+ 15. Kxf2 e6 and Black is a pawn up with a safe position.) (8. Qa4!? {was played against me by IM Andrew Greet in a rapid game back in 2006. However, Black is doing fine after} e6 9. Ngf3 Nd7 (directed against Ne5 ideas) 10. O-O Bd6 11. Bh4 Bh5 12. Bg3 Bg6 13. Bb5 Bxg3 14. hxg3 O-O 15. Rfe1 Rfc8 16. Re3 a6 17. Bxc6 Qxc6 18. Qb3 h6 19. Ne5 Axe5 20. dxe5 b5 by when he has the standard plan of attacking the white queenside pawns.) (8. Qb3 e6 (8... e5!? {is also possible as} Bxf6 gxf6 {gives Black a slight advantage and} 10. Qxd5?? {is definitely not possible because of} Rd8, {regaining the pawn with some advantage.}) 9. Ngf3 Bd6 and Black continues in much the same way with ...Be7 10. O-O Nd7 11. Bf4 Bh5 12. Bg3 Bg6 13. Bxd6 Qxd6 14. Be2 O-O.) (8. Qe2 {is really not a problem. Black simply retreats and goes} Bf5 9. Ngf3 e6 10. Qh4 Bd6 (10...Be7 is also possible) 11. Ng3 O-O 12. Bxd6 Qxd6 13. O-O Rab8 14. Qa4 (M.Narciso Dublan-F.Peralta, Badalona 2009) a6, planning a minority attack on the queenside.) (8. Qc2 h5 9. Bh4 e6 10. Ngf3 Bd6 11. O-O Bg6, again with a reasonable position.)
8...e6 Black has not faced any problems developing his pieces – this means we have a very normal position. The first concrete idea is to neutralize any potential kingside attack.
9. Bh4 (9. h3 h5 nudges Black to follow the correct plan of ...Bg4-h5-Bg6. Indeed, after 10. O-O Bd6 11. Qa4 (or 11. Qb3 O-O 12. Rfe1 Rab8 13. a4 Nd7 14. Ng4 Bg6 15. Bxg6 hxg6 16. Bh4 a6 17. Qf1 b5 18. Bg3 b4 with the advantage for Black in L.Donovan-N.Eliet, Belgian League 2011) Bg6 12. Bxg6 hxg6 13. Rfe1 O-O 14. Ne2 b5! (a common strike) 15. Qc2 Rac8 16. Qd3 b4 17. Nc1 bxc3 18. Rxc3 Qb6 Black's control over the open c-line and White's weak d4-pawn hand Black an easy game, P.Tvarijonas-D.Fridman, Riga 2012.)) (9. O-O {we can see the neutralization plan in action:} h5 10. Re1 Bg6 11. Be2 Bd6 12. Ngf1 Ne4 13. Ne3 (A.Bitalzadeh-S.Ernst, Groningen 2006) O-O 14. Nh4 Na5 15. Nxg6 hxg6 16. Bd3 Nc4 17. Bxe4 dxe4 18. Nb3 Nb2 19. Qc2 Nd3 and Black is much better.))
Bg6 10. Bxd6 {This manoeuvre is an important defensive idea. The idea is to swap off White's menacing light-squared bishop on d3. However, it is usually only a good idea to play ...Bg6 when (a) Black has not castled short and intends to go queenside, or (b) White has played h2-h3, thereby preventing ideas of h3 and Qh4. This is about the only special manoeuvre Black has to remember; everything else is straightforward.} Bh5! 11. Bxd6 (11. O-O {White intends Re1 followed by} Ne5, {but after} Nxg3 12. hxg3 (13. Qc2 {fails to} Nb4 and 13. Bxg6?! hxg6 {plays into Black's hands; he will castle queenside and attack down the h-file.}) Bxd3 14. Qxd3 O-O 15. Rfe1 Rac8 Black is fine.) Qxd6 12. O-O O-O 13. Re1 Rab8 {Intending to begin the queenside offensive.} 14. a4 Nc7 15. Qb1 a6 16. Ne5 Rfe8 17. Nh3 Bg6 18. Nxg6! hxg6 19. Qd3 Nxe5 20. dxe5 Alternatively, 20. Axe5 Rec8 with the idea of ...Re8-Rd6-Nc4.
Rd7 21. Qd4 Rec8 22. Re3 Qb6! 23. Qxb6 Rxb6 Black's position was the easier to play in M.Illescas Cordoba-V.Anand, Linares 1994.
