1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. exd5 cxd5 4. Bd3 Nc6 5. c3 Qc7 6. Qe2 Bg4! 7. f3 Solving the problem of the light-squared bishop. The most popular continuation. White does not want to see Black exchange the perceived 'bad' bishop for a knight that can in the future have influence over the e5-square. Let's look at what happens if White avoids f2-f3. There is one rule here: when Bg3 is threatened, capture the knight on e2: (7. Qb3 {following the rule; Black does not want his bishop back on d7 and with one of the knights gone, he can rest safe in the knowledge that there is one less piece eyeing up the critical e5-square.} Nxe2! 8. Bxe2 (8...e6 9. Qa3 Rc8 is an alternative method of playing) 9. Qxd5 {and now instead of the original recommendation} Bxe2, I now like Rd8 (9... Nc2+ would lose immediately after 10. Kd1 Rxd8 11. Nb5+) 10. Nb5+ Bxb5 11. Bxb5+ Nd7 12. Be3 a6 13. Qa4 Nf6 14. Bd2 Nd5 when Black had easy development in J.Groz-S.Kojima, Cappelle-la-Grande 2011.) (7. O-O {we go} e6 8. Bf4 (8. Qf4 Nxe2! 9. Nxe2 Nf6 10. Nd2 Bd6 11. Nf3 O-O 12. Bg5 Nd7 it's not very clear what White's plan will be, S.Vidovic-E.Dizdarevic, Sibenik 2009; Black, on the other hand, will play for the pawn break ...e6-e5 in order to activate his knights)) (7. h3 Nxe2! (7...h5 is also possible, but after 8 Qb3 Black must capture the knight because of the threatened Bg3) Bxe2 Nxd4 gave Black an easy game in Fan Yun-A.Ostrovsky, Philadelphia 2011.) (7. Qa4 Nxe2! (following the formula) Bxe2 e6 9. Bg5 Be7 10. Bxe7 Nxe7 11. Nd2 O-O 12. Qd1 e5 13. dxe5 Nd4 14. O-O Nxc3 15. bxc3 Qxe5 16. Rc1 Rad8 17. Qe1 Rfe8 and Black already had the upper hand in T.Heinemann-L.Nisipeanu, Hamburg 2012.) (7. Bf4 {looks strange. Black should simply control the f5-square and get on with developing his pieces:} e6 8. O-O h6 9. Nd2 Bd6 10. Nf3 f5 11. Ne5 Nxd3 12. Qxd3 Nf5 13. Nxc6 (or 13. g4 Rfe7 14. Ng3 O-O 15. Ng5 f6, breaking out) 13...Qxc6 14. Nd2 h5 15. Rae1 O-O 16. Nf3 b5 17. h3 g6 18. Qf3 Bg7 19. Nc1 Qc4 20. Kh1 b4 and Black's initiative on the queenside was more dangerous than White's stunted attack in D.Hiermann-G.Neff, Lienz 2011.) (7. Qc2? {Black should} Nxe2 {and neither reply is good due to the potential fork on d4.}) (7. Na3 was played against me once. Obviously Black should deal with the threat in question and play a6! 8. Nc2 e6 9. Bf4 Bd6 10. Bxd6 Qxd6 11. Nf3 h5 12. Ng3 Bg6 13. O-O Nge7 14. Bf4 Nxd3 15. Qxd3 g6 (following the rule of keeping f4-f5 under check) 16. Ne3 and now in M. Larsen-J.Houska, Harstad 2013, I was afraid that after 16...h5 17. Bf5 gxf5 18. Ngxf5 Rxf5 19. Nxf5 exf5 20. Bxf5 the position was not entirely clear, but simply 16... O-O is fine for Black.) (7. Bf4 Qxf4 8. Bxf4 Nxd1 Bxd1 e6 10. Nd2 Bd6 11. h3 Nf6 and Black has no problems in the endgame.) (7. Nd2 e6 8. Bf1 {with the idea of manoeuvring the queen's knight to g3 was an interesting try by GM Rozentalis. As White has, though, wasted some time with the knight, Black should simply retreat the bishop:} h5 9. Nfg3 Bg6 10. Bf4 Nf6 11. Nfh5 Nxh5 12. Nxh5 O-O-O and Black has few problems with his development.))
Nd7 {Now that Black has provoked a weakness, he retreats to safety. The critical question for both sides is when can Black strike out with the thematic and aggressive ...e7-e5.} (7... h5?! {gives White the better position after} Bf4!? Qb6 9. Qb3! (threatening to exchange queens and leave Black with weak doubled pawns) 9... Qxb3 10. axb3.)
Qa3 {This has the rather obvious idea of playing b5 as well as of transferring the knight to e3 via c2.} a6 9. Nc2 e5! (8...a6 9. Qb3 {Black should strike immediately with} e5! 10. Qxd5 e6 11. Qe4 Nf6 12. Qh4 exd4! cxd4 (13...Bxd4 {is inferior on account of} Bxd4 Qxd4 Rd8! {and White has problems defending his pieces; here} 15. Qa4+ {is harmless because of} b5 16. Bxb5 axb5 17. Bxb5+ Nd7 18. Bxd7+ Nxd7 {when the pawns are not worth the piece.}) Qb4+ Kf2 Qd8 (with the threat of winning the queen with...Bg4+) Qxg7 Rg8.) (8...a6 9. O-O {is the only move that dissuades Black from playing his planned} e7-e5. Now I like Nf6 e1 (10. Bg5 should be met with normal development and O-O (10...Bf5 with the standard e5!) e6 Bf4 with the standard Bf6 gxf6 (if Nxg6 gxf6 h5 and Black will soon start an attack on the kingside) Qh6 is slightly better for Black.))
dxe5 { Energetic and effective.
 Bxe5 11. Nd4 Bd6 12. Bf5 {This looks very tempting, but fails to trouble Black.} Qxd3+ Qxd3 Nxf5 Qf5 Be7 Qg4 (221)
15...g6 {This is designed to prevent White from exchanging pieces with Bf4. Instead, Bf4 g6 Bdf4 Ne3 16. Bf4 was equal in S.Khabarov-D.Svetushkin, Paleochora 2011.
17. O-O Ra8 ae8 f5 Ne3 Qh5!} (222) Bxf4}
Bf4 {Obvious, but Black's play is now very energetic and, indeed, White must be careful not to emerge from the opening somewhat worse.} e5! {A good answer. This line has been played by several grandmasters, amongst them world-class players Alexei Dreev and Liviu-Dieter Nisipeanu.} (32) Bg3?! {A little too timid.} Nd6 Qa3 a6 Nc2 Ne7 O-O h5!? {With the threat of a possible ....h5-h4, exposing a weakness on the h2-b8 diagonal. (e5 Black now has plans of controlling the e-file. Bf8!
19.Nd2 Re8 20.Nf5 Bg8 21.Nf4 Nf6 22 Ac1 b5l Black soon won in B.Gonzalez-K.Sasikiran, Mallorca Olympiad 2004.)
